<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Town Council – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Town Council</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How Our Town Operates</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Present an analysis of a current issue, local or global. Interactive: Engage in discussions of general or academic interests.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can present an analysis of civic responsibilities, prompting classmates to share their views on community engagement (e.g., "My analysis shows civic responsibility improves community well-being. What do you think?").
            Can engage in role-play discussions simulating town council meetings to present key points and respond to community suggestions (e.g., "The new park renovations were approved based on citizen feedback. What other suggestions should be implemented?").
            Can summarize classmates' ideas during discussions on local governance, keeping the conversation focused (e.g., "We all agree that voting is a key responsibility of citizens.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 15px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and initiates a quick "Think-Pair-Share" activity. Students are asked: "What do you think are the most important civic responsibilities for citizens in our community?" Students briefly reflect, share with a partner, and then a few volunteers share their ideas with the class. The teacher encourages use of vocabulary like *voting, participation, involvement, responsibilities* and adjectives like *civic, responsible*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary for the day, focusing on the short vowel sound /ɪ/ in words like *public, civic*, and the /e/ sound followed by 'r' in words like *share, serve*. Word stress in multisyllabic words such as "responsibility" and "participation" is also modeled clearly. The teacher also models phrases for asking for opinions (e.g., "What do you think about...?") and sharing viewpoints (e.g., "I believe that citizens should...").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key terms and concepts from the previous lessons related to town governance.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is giving feedback to the town council a form of participation?" (Expected answer: Yes, it is.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does the municipal government provide public services only to a few people?" (Expected answer: No, it provides public services to the whole community.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Town Council".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An authentic audio recording (or a clear transcript presented visually) of a short segment from a local town council meeting or a simulated dialogue discussing a community issue (e.g., a proposed public park project or a new recycling initiative). The genre is formal discussion/dialogue; the register is formal to semi-formal, showcasing target grammar and vocabulary. This material allows students to hear the language in context before actively producing it.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> To express and exchange information and ideas on various topics, including current issues and community affairs, using appropriate language and register, while actively participating in discussions related to local governance and community projects.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the audio (or read the transcript) and answer:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main issue being discussed by the town council members and citizens?" (e.g., "The main issue is improving public services in the community.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Detail questions:</b> "What *suggestions are made* by the citizens?" (passive voice) and "What *decisions were reported* by the council leader?" (reported speech).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice using the Passive voice (e.g., "Decisions *are made* based on community feedback."), Reported speech (e.g., "The mayor *said that new policies would be implemented soon*."), and Modals "can," "should," "must," "may," "might" (e.g., "Citizens *must vote* to have a say in community decisions."). They will integrate vocabulary such as *town council, community, voice, feedback, decisions, public services, citizens, participation, voting, responsibilities, projects, operate, provide, participate, vote, share, decide, express, inform, recommend, civic, responsible, involved*. Pronunciation practice of /ɪ/, /e/ followed by 'r', and word stress will be embedded.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Transformation (5 min):</b> The teacher displays sentences in active voice or direct speech on the board. In pairs, students orally transform them into passive voice or reported speech.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">"The citizens elect the authorities every four years." → "The authorities *are elected* by the citizens every four years." (Passive voice)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">"A local activist said, 'We need more green spaces in our city.'" → "A local activist *said that they needed* more green spaces in their city." (Reported speech)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Focus on clear pronunciation of /ɪ/ in 'citizen' and word stress in 'authorities'.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-Controlled Practice - Modal Debates (6 min):</b> Students work in small groups (3-4). The teacher presents a local issue (e.g., "Should the town council invest more in public transport or in road repairs?"). Each group member must use at least two different modals ("should," "must," "can," "might," "may") to express their opinion or make a suggestion, and provide a reason.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "I think the town council *should prioritize* public transport because it *can reduce* traffic."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "Yes, but we *must also consider* road repairs; some areas *might be dangerous* without them."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Teacher monitors for accurate modal use and pronunciation, particularly of 'share' and 'serve'.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Sharing Viewpoints (6 min):</b> Students are paired up. They are given prompts related to civic responsibilities and community involvement (e.g., "What role *should citizens play* in decision-making?", "How *can feedback be collected* effectively?"). They take turns asking for and sharing opinions, using phrases like "What do you think are important duties for citizens?" and "Citizens *should vote* to make their voices heard." Peer correction is encouraged. Teacher reminds students to use formal language and show respect in dialogue.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">17 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Town Council".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small group role-play (groups of 3-4 students) simulating a "Community Feedback Session" at a town council meeting. Each group receives a specific local issue (e.g., "Improvement of public parks," "Waste management solutions," "Youth involvement in community projects").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students assign roles: 1-2 students are "Town Council Members" (to present initial information and respond to feedback), and 2-3 students are "Concerned Citizens" or "Community Representatives" (to present analysis, share viewpoints, and make suggestions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Town Council Members</b> start by presenting a brief overview of the issue, possibly using passive voice (e.g., "A proposal *was submitted* regarding..."). They also report on previous actions (e.g., "The committee *reported that* initial surveys *were conducted*.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Concerned Citizens/Representatives</b> then present their analysis of the issue, using modals to make recommendations or express opinions (e.g., "My analysis *shows that greater participation could lead* to more sustainable projects. What do you think, Council Members?"). They also ask for opinions (e.g., "What do you think are important duties for citizens to ensure the project's success?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The discussion should flow naturally, with students engaging in respectful dialogue, asking follow-up questions, and summarizing ideas (e.g., "So, we all agree that *voting is a key responsibility* of citizens."). They should use formal language and show respect as practiced in previous stages.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group presents their role-play for 3-4 minutes.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in presenting analyses and viewpoints, engaging interactively in discussions, using target grammar (passive voice, reported speech, modals) and vocabulary related to *town council, public services, civic responsibilities, involvement, decisions, projects*, and demonstrating appropriate pragmatic and sociolinguistic conventions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> The teacher uses an observation checklist to assess students' speaking performance during the role-play. The checklist focuses on their ability to use target language, engage effectively, and maintain appropriate communicative conventions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate actively in the "Community Feedback Session" role-play, presenting their analysis of a community issue, asking for and sharing viewpoints, and responding to suggestions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of /ɪ/ (e.g., *public, civic*), /e/ followed by 'r' (e.g., *share, serve*), and correct word stress in multisyllabic words (*responsibility, participation, authority*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate and accurate use of target vocabulary (e.g., *town council, public services, civic, responsibilities, participation, involvement, decisions*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of passive voice, reported speech, and modals to express ideas and report information.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence of the analysis presented and arguments made during discussions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active and respectful engagement in group discussion, including asking for opinions and summarizing ideas, using formal language.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"During the role-play, did I effectively use the passive voice and modals to share my ideas and make suggestions?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I actively participate, listen respectfully, and try to summarize my classmates' opinions during the discussion?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b> Students reflect on their learning and performance by considering:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part about discussing community issues and engaging in a formal meeting simulation in English?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can I improve my ability to present my ideas and respond to others respectfully in future formal discussions?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking-focused lesson? Were students actively engaged and able to use the target grammar (passive voice, reported speech, modals) and vocabulary effectively in their discussions and role-plays?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed to support students' speaking fluency and accuracy for the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition), especially regarding expressing complex ideas related to community governance? (Perhaps a review of specific formal linking words or sentence structures for written reports.)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson, students will consolidate the vocabulary and grammatical structures (passive voice, reported speech, modals) practiced today in speaking activities by applying them to a writing task. They will compose a formal letter to the town council or a brief report analyzing a local issue and proposing solutions, using the specific terminology and formal register encountered during this lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>